What the Script Does (High-Level)

This document explains the analysis pipeline in plain language.

Step 1: Event Retrieval

For each event and detector, the script retrieves the event GPS time and fetches a strain segment centered on that time using a legacy full window.

If data retrieval fails, the script reports the error and continues with the next event.

Step 2: Ratio Extraction

From each strain segment, the script identifies amplitude peaks and dips and computes ratios in both amplitude and time domains. These ratios reduce raw strain data into scale-independent quantities.

Ratio families include:

Peak-to-Peak

Peak-to-Dip

Dip-to-Dip

Step 3: Constant Matching

Computed ratios are compared against a predefined set of mathematical, physical, and astronomical constants using a numerical tolerance. Matches are counted per constant and per domain.

Step 4: Legacy Full-Window Analysis

The script summarizes constant matches over the full legacy window to provide a broad baseline context around each event.

Step 5: ON/OFF Window Analysis

Short ON windows centered near the event GPS time are compared against OFF windows away from the event. This isolates event-localized structure from background behavior.

Step 6: Null Surrogates

Two null transformations are applied to ON-window strain:

Phase scrambling

Segment shuffling

Each null is processed identically to real data to test robustness of observed structure.

Step 7: Monte Carlo Null Estimation

Using the empirical distribution of ON-window ratios, the script generates Monte Carlo samples to estimate expected constant match counts under a ratio-based null model.

Step 8: Statistical Summaries

Aggregate observed counts are compared against Monte Carlo expectations to produce approximate Z-scores, chi-like statistics, and p-value estimates.

Step 9: Tolerance Robustness

Constant matches are recomputed under multiple tolerances to assess stability of rankings across tolerance bands.

Step 10: Classifier Validation

A Random Forest classifier is trained to distinguish real ON segments from OFF and null segments using constant-match features. Cross-validation and event-grouped splits assess robustness.

Step 11: Output Generation

All results are written to CSV files, with optional plots and a PDF summary report.
